1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,631
Two classes write the same test.

2
00:00:02,631 --> 00:00:05,523
Both classes have an
average of fifty marks.

3
00:00:05,923 --> 00:00:08,870
But in one class, everyone
scored close to fifty.

4
00:00:08,870 --> 00:00:12,241
In the other, marks run from
ten all the way to ninety.

5
00:00:12,691 --> 00:00:15,670
Diya spotted this on the
college notice board today.

6
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:18,261
Same average, very different marks.

7
00:00:18,261 --> 00:00:21,146
How do we put that
difference into one number?

8
00:00:21,646 --> 00:00:24,005
That number is called the mean deviation.

9
00:00:24,005 --> 00:00:26,415
It measures how spread out the marks are.

10
00:00:27,065 --> 00:00:29,627
Let us put the marks on a number line.

11
00:00:29,627 --> 00:00:34,370
Class A first: forty eight, forty
nine, fifty, fifty one and fifty two.

12
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:38,549
Now measure how far each mark
is from the average, fifty.

13
00:00:38,549 --> 00:00:40,703
Two, one, zero, one, two.

14
00:00:40,703 --> 00:00:41,907
In total, six.

15
00:00:42,307 --> 00:00:44,602
Share that total among five students.

16
00:00:44,602 --> 00:00:46,673
Six over five is one point two.

17
00:00:46,673 --> 00:00:49,705
On average, a mark is
just one point two away.

18
00:00:50,105 --> 00:00:53,434
Class B: ten, thirty,
fifty, seventy and ninety.

19
00:00:53,434 --> 00:00:56,763
The gaps are forty, twenty,
zero, twenty, forty.

20
00:00:56,763 --> 00:00:58,988
In total, one hundred and twenty.

21
00:00:59,388 --> 00:01:02,481
One hundred and twenty
over five is twenty four.

22
00:01:02,481 --> 00:01:05,866
Same average, but the spread
is twenty times bigger.

23
00:01:06,516 --> 00:01:08,984
Now the rule, for any list of numbers.

24
00:01:09,384 --> 00:01:10,284
Step one.

25
00:01:10,138 --> 00:01:11,269
Find the centre.

26
00:01:11,269 --> 00:01:15,784
The mean, x bar, is the sum of all
the values, divided by how many there

27
00:01:15,784 --> 00:01:16,684
are.

28
00:01:17,003 --> 00:01:17,903
Step two.

29
00:01:17,824 --> 00:01:21,377
For each value, find its
deviation, x minus x bar.

30
00:01:21,377 --> 00:01:26,041
That is how far it is from the
centre, with a plus or a minus sign.

31
00:01:26,791 --> 00:01:27,691
Step three.

32
00:01:27,593 --> 00:01:28,803
Here is a problem.

33
00:01:28,803 --> 00:01:32,741
Add the deviations, and the pluses
and minuses always cancel to zero.

34
00:01:33,491 --> 00:01:34,391
Step four.

35
00:01:34,345 --> 00:01:35,966
So we ignore the sign.

36
00:01:35,966 --> 00:01:40,494
These bars mean absolute value: the
distance, which is never negative.

37
00:01:41,244 --> 00:01:42,144
Step five.

38
00:01:42,034 --> 00:01:44,323
Add the distances, and divide by n.

39
00:01:44,323 --> 00:01:46,893
That is the mean deviation about the mean.

40
00:01:47,643 --> 00:01:51,222
We can also measure from the
median, the middle value.

41
00:01:51,222 --> 00:01:53,958
Then we write x minus M inside the bars.

42
00:01:54,608 --> 00:01:55,654
Side by side.

43
00:01:55,654 --> 00:01:58,433
Take two, four, six, eight and thirty.

44
00:01:58,433 --> 00:01:59,682
The mean is ten.

45
00:01:59,682 --> 00:02:02,761
Measured from ten, the
mean deviation is eight.

46
00:02:03,161 --> 00:02:05,876
The median, the middle value, is six.

47
00:02:05,876 --> 00:02:09,620
Measured from six, the mean
deviation is six point four.

48
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:11,120
Your turn.

49
00:02:11,076 --> 00:02:13,750
One big value, thirty, sits far away.

50
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:17,446
Which centre does it pull
more, the mean or the median?

51
00:02:17,446 --> 00:02:18,655
Pause and think.

52
00:02:21,855 --> 00:02:22,755
The mean!

53
00:02:22,624 --> 00:02:24,754
Thirty drags the mean up to ten.

54
00:02:24,754 --> 00:02:28,723
The median only looks at the
middle value, so it stays at six.

55
00:02:29,373 --> 00:02:30,404
Remember this.

56
00:02:30,404 --> 00:02:33,814
Mean deviation is the average
distance from the centre.

57
00:02:34,514 --> 00:02:37,046
Now, one worked example
from the NCERT book.

58
00:02:37,396 --> 00:02:42,360
Find the mean deviation about the mean
for six, seven, ten, twelve, thirteen,

59
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:44,028
four, eight and twelve.

60
00:02:44,528 --> 00:02:45,428
Step one.

61
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:48,595
The sum is seventy two,
and there are eight values.

62
00:02:48,595 --> 00:02:50,054
So the mean is nine.

63
00:02:50,804 --> 00:02:51,704
Step two.

64
00:02:51,531 --> 00:02:53,307
Find each distance from nine.

65
00:02:53,307 --> 00:02:56,759
Three, two, one, three,
four, five, one and three.

66
00:02:57,509 --> 00:02:58,409
Step three.

67
00:02:58,256 --> 00:02:59,336
Add the distances.

68
00:02:59,336 --> 00:03:00,757
The total is twenty two.

69
00:03:01,507 --> 00:03:02,407
Step four.

70
00:03:02,343 --> 00:03:03,525
Divide by eight.

71
00:03:03,525 --> 00:03:06,293
The mean deviation is
two point seven five.

72
00:03:07,043 --> 00:03:11,754
So on average, each value sits about
two point seven five away from nine.

73
00:03:12,404 --> 00:03:13,868
Now, the exam trap.

74
00:03:14,218 --> 00:03:16,220
Many students forget the bars.

75
00:03:16,220 --> 00:03:19,950
They add x minus x bar, with
the signs, and get zero.

76
00:03:20,350 --> 00:03:24,731
But zero would mean every value
equals the mean, which is false here.

77
00:03:24,731 --> 00:03:26,893
Every gap must count as positive.

78
00:03:26,893 --> 00:03:29,208
The answer is two point seven five.

79
00:03:29,858 --> 00:03:31,129
Where is this asked?

80
00:03:31,129 --> 00:03:32,029
In JEE Main.

81
00:03:32,007 --> 00:03:35,734
In April twenty twenty three, a paper
gave six observations with their

82
00:03:35,734 --> 00:03:39,503
mean and variance, and asked for
the mean deviation about the mean.

83
00:03:40,103 --> 00:03:41,258
Exam level now.

84
00:03:41,258 --> 00:03:44,878
Each number is multiplied by
three, then five is added.

85
00:03:44,878 --> 00:03:46,786
Find the new mean deviation.

86
00:03:46,786 --> 00:03:47,990
Pause and solve.

87
00:03:53,390 --> 00:03:54,833
The answer is B.

88
00:03:54,833 --> 00:03:56,209
Seven point two.

89
00:03:56,709 --> 00:03:58,113
First, the old data.

90
00:03:58,113 --> 00:04:02,475
The mean is six, and the distances
are four, two, zero, two and four.

91
00:04:02,475 --> 00:04:04,885
So the mean deviation is two point four.

92
00:04:05,385 --> 00:04:08,472
Adding five moves every
value and the mean together.

93
00:04:08,472 --> 00:04:10,248
The distances do not change.

94
00:04:10,748 --> 00:04:14,363
Multiplying by three stretches
every distance three times.

95
00:04:14,363 --> 00:04:18,885
So the new mean deviation is three
times two point four, which is seven

96
00:04:18,885 --> 00:04:19,785
point two.

97
00:04:20,352 --> 00:04:21,892
Here is your recap.

98
00:04:21,892 --> 00:04:23,475
Screenshot this card.

99
00:04:26,675 --> 00:04:28,861
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

100
00:04:29,261 --> 00:04:30,161
One.

101
00:04:29,726 --> 00:04:32,871
Why do we take the absolute
value of each deviation?

102
00:04:33,771 --> 00:04:34,671
Two.

103
00:04:34,238 --> 00:04:38,050
Find the mean deviation about
the mean of one, three and five.

104
00:04:38,950 --> 00:04:39,850
Three.

105
00:04:39,448 --> 00:04:41,888
What does a mean deviation
of zero tell you?

106
00:04:42,988 --> 00:04:45,930
Next time: two batsmen
with the same average.

107
00:04:45,930 --> 00:04:47,840
Which one is more consistent?

108
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:52,157
We square the gaps, and meet the
variance and the standard deviation.
